{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>Benjamin, a wolf, shall tear prey.</b> A wolf is what tears prey. It prophesies that they would be snatchers — ״וַחֲטַפְתֶּם לָכֶם אִישׁ אִשְׁתּוֹ בְּפִלֶגֶשׁ בְּגִבְעָה״ (Judges 21) — and it prophesies about Saul, who would prevail against his enemies on every side, as it says ״וְשָׁאוּל לָכַד הַמְּלוּכָה וַיִּלָּחֶם בְּמוֹאָב וְגוֹ׳״ וּבֶאֱדוֹם וְגוֹ׳ וּבְכֹל אֲשֶׁר יִפְנֶה יַרְשִׁיעַ (1 Samuel 14)." "<b>In the morning he shall devour prey.</b> An expression of spoil and booty — the Targum renders עֲדָאָה — and it also has a likeness in the Hebrew tongue: ״אָז חֻלַּק עַד שָׁלָל״ (Isaiah 33). And it is said about Saul, who stood at the beginning of Israel's blossoming and shining." "<b>And at evening he shall divide spoil.</b> Even when the sun of Israel sets through Nebuchadnezzar, who exiled them to Babylon — <b>he shall divide spoil</b>: Mordecai and Esther, who are of Benjamin, will divide the spoil of Haman, as it says ״הִנֵּה בֵית הָמָן נָתַתִּי לְאֶסְתֵּר.״ And Onkelos translates it about the priests' portion in the consecrated things of the Sanctuary."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:04:20.596800172Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}